h prime for ({interval3}, {interval4}): {result}\") # Output: YES (length 3)\n\n    # Example that differs from problem statement sample but follows design spec (length=2, which is prime)\n    interval5 = Interval(-1, 1)\n    interval6 = Interval(0, 4)\n    result = intersection_service.is_intersection_length_prime(interval5, interval6)\n    print(f\"Intersection length prime for ({interval5}, {interval6}): {result}\") # Output: YES\n    \nexcept (InvalidIntervalError, ValidationError) as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n```\n\n### Command-Line Interface (CLI)\n\n```bash\n# Using the installed console script\nintervalprime 1 3 2 4\n# Output: NO\n\nintervalprime -3 -1 -5 5\n# Output: YES\n\nintervalprime 5 1 2 3\n# Output: Error: Invalid interval: start 5 cannot be greater than end 1. Exiting.\n\n# Or, running directly from module\npython -m intervalprime.controllers.cli_controller 1 3 2 4\n# Output: NO\n```\n\n### HTTP API (Optional)\n\nFirst, make sure you have installed the `api` extra: `pip install \"intervalprime[api]\"`.\nThen, run the API server using uvicorn:\n\n```bash\nuvicorn intervalprime.controllers.api_server:app --reload\n```\n\nThe API will be available, typically at `http://127.0.0.1:8000`.\n\nYou can then send POST requests:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"http://127.0.0.1:8000/intersect\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"interval1\": [1, 3], \"interval2\": [2, 4]}'\n# Response: {\"result\":\"NO\"}\n\ncurl -X POST \"http://127.0.0.1:8000/intersect\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"interval1\": [-3, -1], \"interval2\": [-5, 5]}'\n# Response: {\"result\":\"YES\"}\n\ncurl -X POST \"http://127.0.0.1:8000/intersect\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"interval1\": [5, 1], \"interval2\": [2, 3]}'\n# Response: {\"detail\":\"Invalid interval: start 5 cannot be greater than end 1.\"} (HTTP 400)\n```\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n.\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 pyproject.toml\n\u251c\u