{"task_id": "HumanEval/128", "solution": "def prod_signs(arr: list[int]) -> int | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the sum of magnitudes multiplied by the product of signs for an array of integers.\n    Returns None for an empty array.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return None\n\n    product_of_signs = 1\n    sum_of_magnitudes = 0\n    \n    # Calculate product of signs\n    for num in arr:\n        if num == 0:\n            product_of_signs = 0\n            break  # Short-circuit if any zero is found\n        elif num < 0:\n            product_of_signs *= -1\n        # For positive numbers, product_of_signs *= 1, which doesn't change it\n\n    # Calculate sum of magnitudes\n    for num in arr:\n        sum_of_magnitudes += abs(num)\n\n    return product_of_signs * sum_of_magnitudes"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "def minPath(grid, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the lexicographically minimum path of length k on an N x N integer grid.\n    The path starts at the cell containing 1 and greedily moves to the neighbor with the smallest value.\n    \"\"\"\n    N = len(grid)\n\n    start_row, start_col = -1, -1\n    for r in range(N):\n        for c in range(N):\n            if grid[r][c] == 1:\n                start_row, start_col = r, c\n                break\n        if start_row != -1:\n            break\n\n    path_values = []\n    current_row, current_col = start_row, start_col\n\n    directions = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)] # Up, Down, Left, Right\n\n    for _ in range(k):\n        path_values.append(grid[current_row][current_col])\n        if len(path_values) == k:\n            break\n\n        min_neighbor_value = float('inf')\n        next_row, next_col = -1, -1\n\n        for dr, dc in directions:\n            neighbor_r, neighbor_c = current_row + dr, current_col + dc\n\n            if 0 <= neighbor_r < N and 0 <= neighbor_c < N:\n                neighbor_value = grid[neighbor_r][neighbor_c]\n                if neighbor_value < min_neighbor_value:\n                    min_neighbor_value = neighbor_value\n                    next_row, next_col = neighbor_r, neighbor_c\n        \n        current_row, current_col = next_row, next_col\n\n    return path_values"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "from typing import List, Set, Dict, Tuple, Iterator\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Position:\n    \"\"\"Represents a position (row, column) on the grid.\"\"\"\n    r: int\n    c: int\n\n    def neighbors(self, n: int) -> List[\"Position\"]:\n        \"\"\"Returns in-bounds orthogonal neighbors for a grid of size n.\"\"\"\n        res = []\n        if self.r > 0: res.append(Position(self.r - 1, self.c))\n        if self.r + 1 < n: res.append(Position(self.r + 1, self.c))\n        if self.c > 0: res.append(Position(self.r, self.c - 1))\n        if self.c + 1 < n: res.append(Position(self.r, self.c + 1))\n        return res\n\n    def to_index(self, n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Converts position to a linear index for a grid of size n.\"\"\"\n        return self.r * n + self.c\n\nclass Grid:\n    \"\"\"Models the game grid, providing value and adjacency lookups.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, values: List[List[int]]) -> None:\n        self.values = values\n        self.n = len(values)\n        # Precompute value-to-position mapping for O(1) lookups.\n        self._value_to_pos: Dict[int, Position] = {\n            self.values[r][c]: Position(r, c)\n            for r in range(self.n) for c in range(self.n)\n        }\n\n    def value_at(self, pos: Position) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Returns the value at the given grid position.\"\"\"\n        return self.values[pos.r][pos.c]\n\n    def positions_with_value(self, value: int) -> Position:\n        \"\"\"Returns the position corresponding to a specific cell value.\"\"\"\n        return self._value_to_pos[value]\n\n    def neighbors(self, pos: Position) -> List[Position]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the orthogonal neighbors of a given position.\"\"\"\n        return pos.neighbors(self.n)\n\n    def all_positions(self) -> Iterator[Position]:\n        \"\"\"Iterates over all positions in the grid.\"\"\"\n        for r in range(self.n):\n            for c in range(self.n):\n                yield Position(r, c)\n\n@dataclass\nclass PathResult:\n    \"\"\"DTO for the computed path sequence and visited positions.\"\"\"\n    sequence: List[int]\n    positions: List[Position]\n\n    def to_list(self) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the list of values in the path sequence.\"\"\"\n        return self.sequence\n\n\ndef minPath(grid: List[List[int]], k: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a grid and length k, finds the lexicographically minimum path.\n    This uses a greedy, iterative approach, selecting the smallest available value at each step.\n    \"\"\"\n    _grid_obj = Grid(grid)\n    n = _grid_obj.n\n\n    # The frontier represents all possible positions that could be the current cell in the path.\n    frontier: Set[Position] = set(_grid_obj.all_positions())\n    sequence: List[int] = []\n    # positions_seq tracks the concrete path of positions.\n    positions_seq: List[Position] = []\n\n    for _ in range(k):\n        # Group reachable positions by their cell value.\n        groups: Dict[int, Set[Position]] = {}\n        for pos in frontier:\n            val = _grid_obj.value_at(pos)\n            groups.setdefault(val, set()).add(pos)\n\n        # Choose the minimal value from the current reachable options.\n        min_val = min(groups.keys())\n        selected_positions = groups[min_val]\n\n        # Deterministically pick a representative position from those with the minimal value.\n        rep = min(selected_positions, key=lambda p: p.to_index(n))\n        \n        sequence.append(min_val)\n        positions_seq.append(rep)\n\n        # Update the frontier for the next step: it will consist of all neighbors\n        # of the positions that produced the minimal value in this step.\n        next_frontier: Set[Position] = set()\n        for pos in selected_positions:\n            next_frontier.update(_grid_obj.neighbors(pos))\n        frontier = next_frontier\n        \n    return PathResult(sequence=sequence, positions=positions_seq).to_list()"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "```python\ndef tri(n):\n    \"\"\"Computes the Tribonacci sequence up to index n, with caching.\"\"\"\n    import json\n    import pathlib\n    import os\n\n    # Configuration values for cache path and temporary file suffix.\n    _DEFAULT_CACHE_PATH = pathlib.Path.home() / \".cache\" / \"tribonacci_app\" / \"cache.json\"\n    _TEMP_SUFFIX = \".tmp\"\n\n    def _ensure_dir_local(path: pathlib.Path) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Ensures the parent directory for a given path exists.\"\"\"\n        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    def _read_cache_file_local(path: pathlib.Path) -> dict[int, list[int]]:\n        \"\"\"Reads the cache file and returns its content or an empty dictionary on error.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            if not path.exists():\n                return {}\n            with path.open('r', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n                # Convert string keys from JSON back to integers.\n                return {int(k): v for k, v in json.load(f).items()}\n        except (IOError, json.JSONDecodeError):\n            return {}\n\n    def _write_cache_file_local(path: pathlib.Path, data: dict[int, list[int]]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Writes data to the cache file atomically, silently handling any I/O errors.\"\"\"\n        _ensure_dir_local(path)\n        temp_path = path.with_suffix(path.suffix + _TEMP_SUFFIX)\n        try:\n            with temp_path.open('w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n                # Convert integer keys to strings for JSON serialization.\n                json.dump({str(k): v for k, v in data.items()}, f)\n            os.replace(temp_path, path) # Atomically replace the old cache file.\n        except IOError:\n            pass # Fail silently as per critical requirements.\n        finally:\n            if temp_path.exists():\n                try: os.remove(temp_path) # Clean up temp file on other failures.\n                except OSError: pass\n\n    def _compute_sequence_local(num: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the Tribonacci sequence elements up to the given index num.\"\"\"\n        seq = [0] * (num + 1)\n        seq[0] = 1\n        if num >= 1:\n            seq[1] = 3\n        for i in range(2, num + 1):\n            if i % 2 == 0:\n                seq[i] = 1 + i // 2\n            else:\n                # For an odd index i, (i+1) is even, so tri(i+1) can be computed directly.\n                tri_ip1 = 1 + (i + 1) // 2\n                seq[i] = seq[i - 1] + seq[i - 2] + tri_ip1\n        return seq\n\n    # Load the entire cache content.\n    cache_data = _read_cache_file_local(_DEFAULT_CACHE_PATH)\n    \n    # Search for the smallest cached sequence that covers the requested 'n'.\n    best_cached_n = -1\n    for cached_n_key in cache_data:\n        if cached_n_key >= n:\n            if best_cached_n == -1 or cached_n_key < best_cached_n:\n                best_cached_n = cached_n_key\n\n    if best_cached_n != -1:\n        # A suitable sequence is found; return the relevant part.\n        return cache_data[best_cached_n][:n+1]\n        \n    # If no existing cache entry covers 'n', compute the new sequence.\n    computed_sequence = _compute_sequence_local(n)\n    \n    # Store the newly computed sequence in the cache and persist it.\n    cache_data[n] = computed_sequence\n    _write_cache_file_local(_DEFAULT_CACHE_PATH, cache_data)\n    \n    return computed_sequence\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "def digits(n):\n    \"\"\"Given a positive integer n, return the product of the odd digits.\n    Returns 0 if all digits are even.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Convert n to a string to iterate through its digits, then convert back to int.\n    # Filter for odd digits.\n    odd_digits = [int(char_digit) for char_digit in str(n) if int(char_digit) % 2 == 1]\n\n    # If no odd digits were found, return 0 as per requirements.\n    if not odd_digits:\n        return 0\n    else:\n        # Compute the product of the remaining odd digits.\n        product = 1\n        for d in odd_digits:\n            product *= d\n        return product"}
