{"task_id": "HumanEval/150", "solution": "```python\nfrom math import isqrt\n\ndef x_or_y(n, x, y):\n    \"\"\"Returns the value of x if n is a prime number, otherwise returns y.\n    This implementation uses an optimized trial division method.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 2:\n        return y\n    if n == 2 or n == 3:\n        return x\n    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\n        return y\n\n    i = 5\n    # Optimized trial division: check divisors of the form 6k \u00b1 1\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return y\n        i += 6\n    return x\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/150", "solution": "```python\ndef x_or_y(n, x, y):\n    \"\"\"Returns x if n is prime, y otherwise.\n    Uses a deterministic Miller-Rabin primality test for n.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def is_prime_inline(val):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime using Miller-Rabin test.\n        Assumes val is a non-negative integer.\n        \"\"\"\n        if val <= 1:\n            return False\n        if val <= 3:\n            return True\n        if val % 2 == 0 or val % 3 == 0:\n            return False\n\n        # Miller-Rabin test with deterministic bases for N < 2^64.\n        MILLER_RABIN_BASES = (2, 325, 9375, 28178, 450775, 9780504, 1795265022)\n\n        # Write val-1 as 2^s * d, where d is odd.\n        d = val - 1\n        s = 0\n        while d % 2 == 0:\n            d //= 2\n            s += 1\n\n        for a in MILLER_RABIN_BASES:\n            if a >= val: # Skip bases larger than or equal to the number being tested.\n                continue\n\n            x_mr = pow(a, d, val)\n            if x_mr == 1 or x_mr == val - 1:\n                continue\n\n            is_composite = True\n            for _ in range(s - 1):\n                x_mr = pow(x_mr, 2, val)\n                if x_mr == val - 1:\n                    is_composite = False\n                    break\n            if is_composite:\n                return False\n\n        return True\n\n    if is_prime_inline(n):\n        return x\n    else:\n        return y\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/151", "solution": "```python\ndef double_the_difference(lst):\n    # Computes the sum of squares of odd positive integers from a list.\n    # Ignores negative numbers and non-integers, returning 0 if no qualifying numbers exist.\n    total = 0\n    for item in lst:\n        # Filter for integers that are non-negative.\n        if isinstance(item, int) and item >= 0:\n            # Filter for odd integers and compute their square.\n            if item % 2 != 0:\n                total += item * item\n    return total\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/151", "solution": "```python\ndef double_the_difference(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return the sum of squares of the numbers\n    in the list that are odd. Ignore numbers that are negative or not integers.\n    '''\n    total = 0\n    for num in lst:\n        # Ignore negative numbers.\n        if num < 0:\n            continue\n        # Ignore even numbers; only process positive odds.\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            continue\n        total += num * num\n    return int(total)\n```"}
